{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1PzhT5fXAfuH1T6rpBA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. The file is unchanged and still has its `sorry`.\n\nThe answer is p = 7. The witness is a = 3, r = 1, with b = 1, 2, 3. The hard direction is that no prime p > 7 works. Outline of that argument:\n\n- The b_n form an arithmetic progression mod p with step r ≠ 0, so the powers 1, a, …, a^(p-5) are p−4 distinct residues. So a is nonzero mod p and its order d satisfies d ≥ p−4 with d | p−1.\n- For p > 7 that forces d = p−1, so the powers are the nonzero residues minus a^(-1), a^(-2), a^(-3). The complement {0, a^(-1), a^(-2), a^(-3)} must then be a 4-term arithmetic progression mod p.\n- Scaling reduces that to four cases, each giving a polynomial relation in a^(-1) mod p that has to be ruled out for every prime p > 7.\n\nFormalizing this in Lean means handling order arguments in `ZMod p`, the counting that makes the powers distinct, and a case analysis that depends on p. That is well beyond what I can finish reliably with only `lean` on the command line and no Mathlib sources to consult."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":573,"cache_read_input_tokens":3606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":573,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0560b79d-f82c-4fff-95f4-ed391ae12872","uuid":"b7ee5da2-2987-4a56-93de-42ccfa38ea52","timestamp":"2026-10-14T03:11:44.679Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1Pzh7zeKz7oV5wdYgKY"}
